Core Difference: Deployment Model

============================
GitLab (Push-based)
Deployment is triggered from GitLab CI/CD pipelines.
The runner (or pipeline job) pushes code/artifacts to target servers.
Typically uses:
SSH
Kubernetes API
Deployment scripts (Ansible, etc.)

Flow:

Code commit → Pipeline runs → GitLab Runner → Push to server


Nolio (Pull-based)
Uses agents installed on target servers.
Agents poll the Nolio server for deployment instructions.
When a deployment is triggered:
Agent pulls artifacts and executes locally.
No inbound connection needed

✅ Option 1: GitLab Runner on Target Server (Closest to Pull Model)
Install a GitLab Runner directly on the server.
Configure it with a specific tag.
Jobs run on that server itself.


Option 3: Hybrid with Artifact Repositories
Pipeline pushes artifact to repo (e.g., Nexus, S3)
Servers pull from repo via scheduled jobs


===============

Traditional GitLab (push)
Runner sits somewhere else
Connects via SSH → pushes to server
Runner-on-server (pull-like)
Server itself runs the job
No inbound connection needed

👉 This solves:

Firewall restrictions
No SSH access required
More secure environments


=======================================
1. Security
Runner has direct access to server
Be careful:
Limit who can trigger pipelines
Use protected branches

2. Tag Control (Very Important)
Tags ensure:
Job goes to correct server
Without tags → wrong server might pick job

3. Scaling
Multiple servers = multiple runners
Each server = its own execution node

4.✅ When to Use This Approach

Use Runner-on-server when:

🔐 No inbound SSH allowed
🏢 Strict on-prem firewall
🔄 Want agent-like behavior (like Nolio)
⚡ Need local execution

You have many dynamic environments (hard to manage runners)

Flow:

Deployment triggered → Agent checks → Pulls package → Deploys


